body:
A random-quote widget is a perfect first project with GratisAPI. It needs one endpoint, a little JavaScript, and no backend at all, yet it teaches fetching, parsing, and rendering in one go.

The quotes collection lives at /api/quotes/index.json and returns an object with a quotes array. Fetch it once, pick a random element, and drop it into the page:

async function showQuote() {
  const res = await fetch("https://domthedeveloper.github.io/GratisAPI/api/quotes/index.json");
  const data = await res.json();
  const quotes = data.quotes;
  const q = quotes[Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length)];
  document.getElementById("quote").textContent = q.text;
  document.getElementById("author").textContent = q.author;
}

showQuote();

The expression Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length) produces a valid random index into the array. Because the whole collection arrives in a single request, you do not need to hit the network again to show another quote. Wire a button to a function that picks a new random element from the array you already have:

document.getElementById("next").addEventListener("click", () => {
  const q = quotes[Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length)];
  // update the DOM here
});

This is a real advantage of GratisAPI's full-dataset design. A widget backed by a paginated API would need a fresh request per quote, but here one download powers unlimited shuffles.

To make it robust, keep the fetched array in a variable at a scope both functions can see, and add a try/catch so a network failure shows a friendly fallback message rather than nothing. For a finishing touch, style the quote with CSS and fade it in on change. The entire widget fits in a single HTML file you can embed anywhere, needs no keys, and costs nothing to run, which is exactly the spirit of a gratis, static API.
